  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several common ways to produce hydrogel microparticles (HMPs). Across all methods, a hydrogel precursor is first shaped into small droplets or patterned features, then the material is crosslinked and washed to yield discrete particles.\n\nTwo approaches rely on emulsions. In batch emulsions, the precursor is added to oil and mixed by vortexing, creating many droplets with a broad range of sizes. In microfluidic emulsions, oil and precursor meet in a microchannel, generating more regular droplets in a controlled flow before crosslinking, suggesting better size uniformity.\n\nA second group uses lithography. Imprint lithography, photolithography, and flow lithography all use UV light with molds, masks, or flow channels to define particle shapes, followed by washing to release the formed microparticles. These methods appear to emphasize geometric control rather than simple droplet formation.\n\nThe last two methods are electrohydrodynamic spraying and mechanical fragmentation. In electrohydrodynamic spraying, a syringe pump and electrically driven emitter produce a spray of droplets into a collecting dish, which are then crosslinked. In mechanical fragmentation, a bulk hydrogel is physically broken into particles. Overall, the figure compares fabrication routes that differ mainly in how precisely they control particle size and shape.",
